A search is compiled, then answered or refused
A client asks the tenant's face for a search. The compiler checks every
parameter against what the tenant declared. If all are declared it produces
an envelope predicate, which the database answers as one indexed query with a
page cursor, and the client gets a bundle and a link to the next page. If any
parameter is not declared the search is refused by name and the database is
never asked, because a silently dropped filter would answer a narrower
question while looking like an answer.
